index=f'''# Final build · round 3 of 3

The demonstrated tank/floor intersection is repaired. Deliberate removal of the tower's remaining valid foundations produces complete settled rubble and physical neighbor damage. Smooth time inspection and exact recorded restoration are preserved. The final 91-second payload is {ret['historyBytes']:,} bytes ({ret['historyBytes']/1024**2:.1f} MiB), {reduction*100:.1f}% below build 02.

Open [the visual gallery](GALLERY.html) first. Full acceptance boundaries are machine-readable in [acceptance.json](checks/acceptance.json).

## Identity and repository state

- Runtime SHA-256: `{runtime['sha256']}`.
- Complete source-manifest SHA-256: `{{{{SOURCE_MANIFEST_SHA256}}}}`.
- Existing checkout: `/Users/drewgoddyn/projects/blind-3d-world-astra-build-loop`.
- Branch: `main`; baseline: `186001a2f22b98f3c127a535f742e1eca1935343`.
- Implementation remains uncommitted in the original worktree. Nothing was pushed, published or deployed.
- Build 01 and build 02 archives are preserved unchanged; hashes and final ZIP validation appear in `PACKAGE.json` and the adjacent `build-03-verification.json`.
- All 35 build 02 source hashes matched at round start. `SOURCE_DIFF.patch` is the focused source/configuration/test/tooling diff against that snapshot; the package builder applies it to a temporary copy and verifies every resulting hash.

## Run and controls

From the packaged `source/` directory: `npm ci`, then `npm start`. Open `http://127.0.0.1:4173`. Node 26.3.0 was used; Node 22+ is documented. Dependencies are pinned to Three 0.180.0 and Playwright 1.55.1. The procedural seed is 85173. No external scene assets are needed. Dependencies themselves are excluded from the archive.

Left drag orbits; right drag pans; wheel zooms. Drag the ball to swing. A/D rotate the crane, W/S change cable length, arrows add camera-relative momentum. B/C choose ball/charges. Gold structural targets accept up to 6 charges, fired 0.18 simulation seconds apart. Slow or held Space gives 10% speed. R/rewind reverses; the slider scrubs; Play follows retained history. Rebuild traverses all recorded history. Reset view changes the camera. Accepted historical input replaces the future; rejected input preserves it. Fractional intervention resumes from the preceding physical record, at most 1/60 second earlier.

## Contact and support outcome

The old center-footprint search allowed a rotated shell through a slab. Tank and structural slabs now use common rotated bounds and physical separation, including roof tiles. The tank envelope fits shell bottom through roof top; no renderer correction lifts it. Unsupported edge overhangs tip, and either face of a flipped slab can support sleep. Contact loss remains recorded even while a body is awake.

The unchanged build 02 replay still produces 268 intersections out of 6,305 shell samples. The final same-input replay plus two edge/support-removal cases and an upright outer-edge case produce zero overlapping frames over 5,344 fixed steps. Independent Three geometry checks inset both envelopes 0.0001 world units, with numerical epsilon 1e-12. Final shell samples are also clear. The final recording's exact accepted controls separately pass another 1,569-step interval and all 6,305 endpoint samples; the reproduced tank state differs by at most 1.6e-13 from the captured state. This checks the clear final close-up without hiding approximate debris contact. The original captured endpoint remains moving on moving support; it is not forced asleep.

Detached visible columns release their old constraints. Healthy beams retain connected movement. Resting slabs validate a contact path to the ground or standing structure and wake if it disappears. A tilted support may have a higher center; center-height ordering is not used as a proxy for support. Six outer tower-foundation charges leave 33 standing bays and three fully healthy foundations in the action-only diagnostic. Removing those remaining three foundations yields 99 resting bays, unchanged numerically from 30 to 40 seconds, with {len(collapse['neighborFailures'])} actual neighboring hotel-bay failures. The original elevated remnant has a valid modeled load path; deliberately removing it produces a complete collapse.

## Resource comparison

| Round | 91s payload | Collapse p95 | Sample maximum | 91s tonnage | Created identities |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
{rows}

Same 1440 × 900 viewport, DPR 1, High quality, M2 Pro with 10 logical cores and 32 GiB RAM, hardware-accelerated ANGLE Metal, headless Chrome 149. Same input schedule: bank 6 foundations at 0s, tower 6 outer foundations at 4s, parking 6 at 65s, tank at 68s; end 91s. Physical outcomes differ because support/contact changed, so payload savings are reported alongside destruction. Build 01 has lower fidelity and is not an equivalent target. All 5,483 records remain.

Profiling identified debris simulation as the dominant elapsed method cost. Bounds caching and removal of unused persistent ages reduce repeated work; exact XOR deltas reduce each changed field from 20 to 12 bytes. Node method timing is diagnostic, separate from browser FPS. Chrome's final collapse sample has {perf['samples'][1]['count']} frames, p95 {perf['samples'][1]['p95Ms']:.1f}ms and maximum {perf['samples'][1]['maxMs']:.1f}ms. Final profiling, browser measurement and recording ran sequentially. Small Node/Chrome floating-point water-flow differences remain disclosed.

## Verification

- `npm test`: 47 passed, 0 failed. 39 prior behavioral intentions retained; 8 regressions added. One velocity fixture observes the pre-contact boundary because new edge impacts occur earlier; its equality and tolerance remain.
- `npm run check`: syntax, pinned named Three imports, import-map ordering, and no remote scene assets passed.
- Contact baseline/final diagnostics, action-only collapse/support evidence, temporal reproductions, exact repeated fractional rendering and quality invariance passed.
- 91s early/late hashes restore exactly; actual Rebuild crosses the entire retained run to the pristine hash. The 47-test coverage includes slot reuse, score, rejected actions, ordered same-time input and replacement futures.
- Visible GPU-input hashes restore exactly; final GPU pixels are not byte-compared. Hidden spent-charge transforms retain non-rendered bookkeeping.
- One independent reviewer and bounded follow-ups found lateral-impact damage, overhang stability and inverted-slab sleep regressions. All were repaired. The inverted-face correction has direct builder regression coverage. The later captured settling regression has its own exact-input test and bounded support-graph follow-up.

Portable checks: `node scripts/contact-evidence-round3.mjs`, `node scripts/collapse-evidence-round3.mjs`, `node scripts/temporal-evidence-round3.mjs`. The optional contact `--baseline` needs the preserved build 02 source. Browser drivers require an isolated `agent-browser` session; commands are in `source/README.md`. The Python capture wrapper optionally uses the locally installed product-demo helper; direct JavaScript driver/agent-browser recording is also documented.

## Media and remaining limits

Ten stills and three continuous recordings show partial damage, connected motion, full tower rubble, actual neighbor damage, tank rupture/contact/support loss, and temporal intervention/restoration. Camera-only framing adjustments, actual speed-control changes and automation are logged. The temporal clip includes at least 6 uninterrupted fixed-camera slow-motion seconds. Pristine/restored stills use matched framing; a whole-city final overview is included.

The recording browser needed `--disable-frame-rate-limit` after its compositor stopped delivering frames, including in a fresh session before recording. A moving/held/moving recorder preflight verifies the workaround. The app clock and video playback speed are unchanged; performance measurements retain default browser frame limiting. Shared recorder maintenance is outside this final assignment.

The builder inspected all final stills and selected decoded frames, checked full-file decode and capture cadence. Full normal-speed viewing of each delivered recording was not completed; the media remains for the human's final visual judgment.

This remains coarse game physics: grouped architecture, conservative bounds, no universal moving-pair collision solver, possible rubble overlap, and some long-lived connected motion. Water is particles with momentum exchange. Steel bending, concrete/rebar failure, billboard dust and a convincing whole-tower lean remain partial relative to the original ambition. 91 simulation seconds is the longest tested run. Memory keeps growing; payload excludes JS objects, renderer allocations and event traces. Short hardware-accelerated frame samples do not guarantee arbitrary sustained 60 fps.

This is the final round. The result is delivered for assessment and freeze; no round 4 is initiated.
'''
